<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Oro Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Oro is a coastal province in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, with its capital at Machala. It is a major producer of bananas and shrimp and has gold mines in its uplands, which give it its name. During the war of 1941 Peruvian forces invaded and occupied the province until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-protocol">Rio de Janeiro Protocol</a> of 1942.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-oro-province.jpg" alt="El Oro Province" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Otzi2007 at Italian Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramic_view_of_Zaruma_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>El Oro is the southernmost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>'s coastal provinces. To the north and east it borders the provinces of Guayas, Azuay and Loja, and to the west and south it is limited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> and the Peruvian Tumbes Region. It is divided into 14 cantons and includes the Jambelí Islands, the island of Santa Clara and the petrified forest of the Puyango River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> inhabited the area when Spanish settlement began in 1549. The Spanish found gold there, mined it and carried it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The mining was done by Indigenous slaves held captive mainly in the late 16th and 17th centuries, in the village of Zaruma, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> granted the title Villa de Sant Antonio del Zerro de Oro de Zaruma. An earthquake in January 1749 destroyed the mining area, and a local rebellion stopped the mining until the Spanish agreed to let the native people benefit from it. Spain imported gold from the area until independence in 1820.</p><p>After 1820 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> emerged as an independent nation. In 1882 the province of El Oro was formed from the villages of Zaruma, Machala and Santa Rosa, with Zaruma as capital, and was named for its gold. Two years later the capital moved to Machala. Mining continued under companies that now paid taxes on the gold they took. As gold mining fell in value, the province became the major banana-growing area of Ecuador. Peru invaded and occupied most of it in the Ecuadorian-Peruvian War of 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The economy rests mainly on exports of bananas and shrimp, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> as other crops. The population was 600,659 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2010, of whom 81.6 percent were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a>, 7.8 percent white and 6.9 percent Afro-Ecuadorian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “El Oro Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In July 1941 Peruvian forces invaded <b>El Oro</b> province and defeated the much smaller Ecuadorian army at the Battle of Zarumilla; the Rio de Janeiro Protocol of January 1942, guaranteed by Argentina, Brazil, Chile and the United States, assigned most of the disputed Amazon to Peru.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Military</a></li></ul></section>
